  "notes": "West leads the ♥Q against 3NT. South appears to have 10 tricks (the ♠A, 2 heart tricks, the ♦A, and 6 club tricks). The only possible problem is that clubs are breaking badly.\n\nSouth must win the opening lead. Ducking is wrong as a spade or diamond switch would not be welcome.\n\nSouth will be in trouble if he cashes the ♣A and ♣K, and finds clubs breaking 4-1. South can surrender a club trick, but there will be no way back to dummy to enjoy the long clubs.\n\nSouth can guard against a 4-1 club break by ducking a club at trick 2. South can win any return and has a club remaining in his hand to reach the dummy. The defenders' remaining clubs will fall under dummy's honours.\n\nSouth doesn't mind losing a trick in clubs as he only needs 5 tricks in that suit. This is an example of what is known as a safety play. A safety play is a precaution taken to guard against an unfavorable break (such as the 4-1 club break in this hand).",
